cef-static is the experimental source-build port, NOT the hybrid cef wrapper port.
It has no binary-distribution/shared-engine fallback. A failed source build fails
installation. Native x64 Linux or Windows, Release engine configuration only.

    find_package(cef-static CONFIG REQUIRED)
    target_link_libraries(app PRIVATE CEF::static)
    cef_static_deploy_resources(app)

Include public C API headers, for example "include/capi/cef_app_capi.h".
Call cef_api_hash(CEF_API_VERSION, 0) before other C API entry points, and implement
cef_execute_process in every child process as in the controlled test fixture.
The ordinary C++ libcef_dll_wrapper is NOT ABI-compatible with this package and
must NOT be linked into the same program.

The source integration also provides CEF::cpp: a complete upstream C++ client
translation layer built with the consumer toolchain. Link that target instead
of calling Chromium's C++ implementations through CEF::static. Its private
client headers and C++ symbol identities are propagated by the target; engine
calls and callbacks use the public C ABI. The client does not import Chromium's
STL, scoped_refptr calling convention, or C++ object ownership across that ABI.

Windows consumers must use /MT, matching the release engine, not /MTd or /MD.
The OS Win32 DLLs remain dynamic. Linux system libraries (libc, X11, NSS, etc.)
remain platform dependencies. This is a static CEF engine, not a statically
linked Linux operating system. ICU, locale packs, resource packs and snapshots
remain data files next to the executable. No libcef.dll/libcef.so is deployed.

The CI fixture loads only a fixed local document with sandbox disabled. Its
security settings are NOT suitable for production/untrusted web content.
Sandbox, GPU functionality and the ordinary C++ wrapper are not certified here.
See the build receipt and repository status before using an experimental build.
